insert into scout_faq (sport, lang, question, answer) values
(null, 'en', 'What''s the right age to start organized sports, and does starting early guarantee an advantage?', 'Most sport scientists recommend a broad, playful introduction to movement between roughly ages 4 and 8, followed by "sampling" several sports through ages 8-12, rather than picking one sport immediately. Starting a specific sport at 5 does not reliably produce a better athlete at 15 or 18. What predicts long-term success is quality of movement experience, coaching, and enjoyment over time, not the calendar age of the first practice.'),
(null, 'en', 'Should my child specialize in one sport, or keep playing multiple?', 'For most children under 12-14, playing multiple sports is associated with fewer overuse injuries, better overall athleticism, and lower burnout rates, and it does not put them behind athletes who specialized earlier. The American Academy of Pediatrics reports that athletes who specialize before age 14 experience overuse injuries at roughly twice the rate of multi-sport peers. Early specialization (before puberty) is genuinely necessary in only a handful of sports where competitive success is tied to pre-puberty body types and skill windows, most notably gymnastics and figure skating; it is not required, and is often counterproductive, in soccer, basketball, tennis, hockey, baseball, and most team sports.'),
(null, 'en', 'What is "peak height velocity" (PHV) and why do coaches talk about it?', 'PHV is the point during puberty when a child is growing fastest (often ages 11-13 for girls, 13-15 for boys, though it varies widely). Around PHV, coordination can temporarily dip (often called "adolescent awkwardness"), injury risk rises, and relative differences in size and strength between same-age kids are at their largest. Many development models (LTAD, USA Hockey''s ADM, U.S. Soccer''s pathway) use PHV rather than birth-year age to decide when to introduce heavier strength training, higher-intensity competition, or position specialization. A child who is small or "behind" at 13 has often not yet hit their growth spurt and can change dramatically by 15-16.'),
(null, 'en', 'How much training is too much? What are the signs of overtraining or burnout?', 'A commonly cited guideline is that weekly hours of organized sport-specific training should not exceed a child''s age in years (e.g., a 12-year-old topping out around 12 hours/week across all training and games), with at least one to two days off per week from that sport and two to three months off per year from single-sport, intensive training. Warning signs include recurring pain that doesn''t resolve with normal rest, declining performance despite more practice, loss of enthusiasm for a sport they used to love, sleep or appetite changes, and irritability specifically around practice or games. These are signals to reduce volume or vary activity, not to push harder.'),
(null, 'en', 'What''s the general path from youth sports to college to professional or elite competition?', 'Across most sports the pathway looks like: (1) recreational/house leagues in early childhood, (2) travel, club, or select teams from roughly ages 9-13 as skill and commitment increase, (3) high school and/or elite club competition from 14-18 where recruiting exposure happens, (4) college athletics (NCAA Division I, II, III or NAIA) as the primary next step for the large majority of athletes who continue past high school, and (5) professional, national-team, or Olympic pathways for a very small fraction who reach that ceiling, sometimes bypassing college entirely (common in soccer, hockey, tennis, and baseball; rare in basketball and most other NCAA sports). Fewer than 2% of high school athletes in most sports go on to play at the NCAA Division I level, and the percentage that turns professional is a fraction of a percent.'),
(null, 'en', 'What is the difference between NCAA D1, D2, D3, and NAIA, and does the division matter?', 'Division I programs are generally the largest, most competitive, and offer the most full athletic scholarships; Division II offers athletic scholarships but with smaller budgets and roster sizes; Division III offers no athletic scholarships (though academic/need-based aid is common) and emphasizes academics alongside sport; NAIA schools are typically smaller private colleges that offer athletic scholarships and somewhat more flexible eligibility rules than the NCAA. "Better" depends on fit: a talented athlete who is not a top recruit nationally will often get more playing time, a better relationship with coaches, and comparable or better financial aid at a D2, D3, or NAIA program than by chasing a marginal D1 roster spot.'),
(null, 'en', 'When are college coaches legally allowed to contact my child?', 'Rules vary by sport, but under NCAA rules most sports allow coaches to begin sending recruiting communications (calls, emails, texts) starting June 15 after a student''s sophomore year of high school, or September 1 of junior year for some sports; men''s ice hockey is a notable exception, allowing contact from January 1 of sophomore year. There is no restriction on the athlete or family contacting a coach first, at any age, and doing so (via email with video/stats) is a normal, encouraged part of the process well before those official contact dates.'),
(null, 'en', 'Do private coaches, showcases, or expensive travel programs guarantee a scholarship?', 'No individual coach, camp, or club can guarantee a scholarship or roster spot; recruiting decisions are made by college coaching staffs based on need, budget, and fit. Showcases, ID camps, and reputable club circuits are valuable because they concentrate college coaches in one place, not because attending one is itself a credential. Families should treat paid "recruiting services" and specific promises of scholarships or roster spots with skepticism, and verify any camp''s actual college-coach attendance before paying for it.'),
(null, 'en', 'How much does competitive club/travel sports typically cost, and is it worth the money?', 'Elite club sports commonly run families several thousand to well over ten thousand dollars per year once club fees, travel, hotels, private lessons, and equipment are included, and costs generally rise sharply as a child moves into showcase-level or shoe-circuit programs. Whether it''s "worth it" depends on the goal: if the goal is a specific elite pathway (Division I scholarship, professional track), the investment is a reasonable bet only if the child is already performing at a level that makes that outcome plausible by the mid-teens; if the goal is enjoyment, fitness, and life skills, a less expensive program usually delivers the same benefit. Families should budget as if the higher levels of exposure are optional upgrades, not requirements, especially before high school.'),
(null, 'en', 'Should we consider homeschooling or an online school to free up time for training?', 'Some elite-track families do move to homeschool, online school, or flexible-schedule academies to accommodate travel and training volume, and it can work well for a highly self-motivated teenager with a clear, realistic elite plan. It is a significant tradeoff, though: it reduces in-person peer and extracurricular life, requires real discipline to keep up academically (which still matters greatly for NCAA eligibility and any non-sport backup plan), and is generally not necessary before the high-school years even for very committed young athletes.'),
(null, 'en', 'At what age is it appropriate to start structured strength training or weightlifting?', 'Pediatric sports-medicine guidance generally supports supervised, technique-focused resistance training starting around age 7-8, using bodyweight and light-load, high-rep movements; there is no evidence that appropriately supervised strength training stunts growth, a persistent myth. What changes with age is intensity and load: heavier, more sport-specific strength and power work is typically introduced after peak height velocity, once growth plates are further along and movement patterns are well established, usually mid-teens.'),
(null, 'en', 'How much do nutrition and sleep actually matter for a young athlete''s development?', 'Considerably more than most families assume: growing, training teenagers generally need 8-10 hours of sleep to support recovery and growth hormone release, and inadequate sleep is linked to higher injury rates and slower skill acquisition. Nutritionally, the main practical issue for most young athletes isn''t exotic supplements but simply eating enough total calories and protein to support both normal growth and training load; under-fueling (sometimes unintentional) is a common, under-diagnosed cause of plateaued performance, mood changes, and injury in youth athletes, especially in leaner-body-type sports like distance running, gymnastics, and figure skating.'),
(null, 'en', 'What is the NCAA transfer portal, and does it affect a high schooler who hasn''t gone to college yet?', 'The transfer portal is the system current college athletes use to signal they''re open to transferring schools; as of recent rule changes, athletes can transfer multiple times without losing eligibility if they remain in good academic standing, and each sport has specific notification windows (for example, men''s and women''s basketball open a window in April; football uses a January 2-16 window). It affects incoming high school recruits indirectly: heavy portal activity means some rosters fill spots with experienced transfers rather than incoming freshmen, so it''s worth asking a recruiting coach directly how many freshman spots they expect to have open versus fill through the portal.'),
(null, 'en', 'What is NIL (name, image, likeness), and does it apply to my child before college?', 'NIL allows athletes to earn money from their name, image, and likeness (sponsorships, social media, camps, autographs); at the college level this has grown substantially, especially after the 2025 House v. NCAA settlement authorized schools to share athletics revenue directly with athletes (Division I schools distributing up to roughly $20.5 million per year in the 2025-26 season). At the high school and youth level, NIL rules are a state-by-state patchwork rather than a single national standard, so what a family''s child can legally earn depends on their state high school athletic association''s specific rules; families should check their state association before pursuing any paid deal.'),
(null, 'en', 'What''s appropriate parent behavior on the sideline, and why does it matter for development?', 'Sport psychologists consistently find that youth athletes perform and enjoy sport more when parents stick to encouragement and let coaches coach, avoid instructing or correcting from the sideline during play, and keep post-game car-ride conversation light unless the child brings it up first. Coaches and recruiters also notice parent behavior directly: college coaches routinely cite a demanding or confrontational sideline parent as a real factor in passing on an otherwise talented recruit, since it signals potential problems managing the parent relationship for four years.'),
(null, 'en', 'Should my child work with a sports psychologist or mental performance coach?', 'It can be genuinely useful at any competitive level, not just elite ones, particularly for athletes dealing with performance anxiety, perfectionism, a difficult injury recovery, or a slump that doesn''t respond to more physical practice. It is not a sign that something is "wrong" with the child; many national-team and professional athletes use one as a normal part of training, the same way they''d use a physical therapist.'),
(null, 'en', 'What''s the difference between a highlight video and a full skills/game video for recruiting?', 'A highlight video (best plays only, usually 2-3 minutes) is meant to grab initial interest quickly and is often the first thing sent cold to a coach; it should not be a player''s only video. Coaches evaluating seriously want unedited or lightly-edited full-game or extended-skills footage that shows decision-making, effort on both sides of the ball, and consistency, not just a highlight reel''s best five minutes. Most recruiting guides recommend having both, and updating them at least once per season.'),
(null, 'en', 'How can a family tell if a program, camp, or "recruiting service" is legitimate versus a scam or low-value product?', 'Legitimate programs are usually verifiable: real college coaches listed as attending a camp can be confirmed directly with that college''s athletic staff or through the school''s own recruiting-camp page, and legitimate clubs/circuits are affiliated with recognized governing bodies (US Soccer/ECNL/MLS NEXT, USA Hockey, USTA, AAU, USA Basketball, etc.). Red flags include vague promises of guaranteed scholarships or roster spots, pressure to pay quickly, no verifiable track record of alumni actually playing at the claimed level, and services that are difficult to distinguish from simple video-editing or database-entry fees.'),
(null, 'en', 'Is it worth paying for an independent recruiting consultant or service?', 'For families unfamiliar with the process, a reputable consultant can save real time by organizing outreach, building a target-school list matched to the athlete''s actual level, and coaching communication with coaches; for families who are already comfortable navigating email, stats platforms (UTR, MaxPreps, etc.), and NCAA rules, a lot of that value can be replicated for free. The clearest sign of value is a consultant who is transparent about the athlete''s realistic level rather than one who flatters every client into believing they''re a high-major prospect.'),
(null, 'en', 'How do injuries like ACL tears or concussions affect a young athlete''s recruiting or next steps?', 'A single well-managed injury with a full, medically-cleared recovery rarely ends a serious athlete''s recruiting prospects, especially if it happens before or during the recruiting window rather than senior year; many D1 athletes have at least one significant injury in their history. What matters most to coaches is documented full recovery, return-to-play clearance, and — especially post-ACL — objective testing (strength symmetry, hop tests) showing the athlete is genuinely back to form, not just "cleared on paper." Repeated concussions raise more serious long-term-health questions that should be driven by medical guidance first and athletic ambition second.'),
(null, 'en', 'What is redshirting, and when does it make sense?', 'Redshirting means a college athlete sits out competition for a season (usually to develop physically or adjust academically) without using up a year of NCAA eligibility, most common in football and to a lesser extent basketball and hockey (where players are often older on entry anyway). It''s typically the coaching staff''s decision, not the family''s, and tends to make the most sense for athletes who are talented but physically underdeveloped relative to the college level, or coming off an injury — it is not a penalty, though families should ask directly during recruiting how a program tends to use it.'),
(null, 'en', 'Should we consider a prep school or postgraduate ("PG") year before college?', 'A prep or postgrad year — common in basketball, hockey, football, and lacrosse — can help a physically or academically underprepared athlete add a year of development, additional showcase exposure, and sometimes NCAA-qualifying coursework before enrolling in college. It''s most useful for a borderline recruit who needs one more year to reach a target level or grade requirement, and least useful (and expensive) for an athlete who is already being recruited at the level they want; it also delays college graduation timing by a year, which matters for some families'' academic and financial planning.'),
(null, 'en', 'What''s the real chance of "walking on" to a college team without being recruited?', 'Walking on is possible and happens every year, but roster spots are limited and walk-ons are almost always athletes who were already performing at a level close to that team''s recruited class, just without a scholarship offer, not a backup plan for someone unrecruited at any level. A realistic walk-on plan usually involves direct communication with the coaching staff well before enrolling (not just showing up to tryouts cold) and a program where the family has verified the roster actually keeps walk-on spots open.'),
(null, 'en', 'How does Title IX affect opportunities for girls in sports, including scholarships?', 'Title IX requires federally funded schools to provide roughly proportional athletic opportunities and scholarship dollars to male and female students relative to their enrollment, which in practice has meant broad expansion of girls'' and women''s college sports (and, at many schools, more total scholarship dollars available per female roster spot in some sports than in comparably-sized men''s programs, because men''s football rosters are large). Families of girls in sports should know that this often makes certain sports — cross country, track, swimming, and rowing in particular — a genuinely more accessible scholarship path relative to team size than equivalent boys'' opportunities.'),
(null, 'en', 'What''s the difference between an athletic scholarship and academic/need-based financial aid, and can they combine?', 'Athletic scholarships are awarded and controlled by a coach/athletic department (full or partial, and "equivalency sports" like soccer, tennis, baseball, and hockey are allowed to split scholarship money across a roster, unlike "headcount sports" such as football and basketball at the D1 level, which award only full scholarships). Academic merit aid and need-based financial aid are awarded separately by the school''s financial aid office and, at most schools, can be combined with a partial athletic scholarship, so a family''s total cost is often lower than the athletic award alone suggests — this is a key reason Division III (no athletic scholarships at all) or Ivy League (no athletic scholarships) schools can still end up affordable for strong students.'),
(null, 'en', 'When does it make sense to hire a private skills coach versus relying only on team practice?', 'Private coaching tends to add the most value once a child has the attention span and basic movement competency to benefit from individualized feedback (commonly middle-school age and up) and when a specific, identifiable weakness (a jump shot, a serve toss, a first touch) isn''t getting individual attention in team practice. It adds the least value, and risks burnout, when it''s layered on top of an already-full team and travel schedule purely because other families are doing it; a good private coach will often say so directly if a child doesn''t need more volume.'),
('Soccer', 'en', 'When should a young player move from recreational to club/travel soccer?', 'There''s no fixed age, but many clubs offer meaningful travel options starting around U9-U10, once a child has basic ball skills and wants more structured competition. Moving too early, purely for status, can backfire if it reduces free play and touches on the ball; moving too late can limit exposure to better coaching and competition once a child is genuinely ready for it. The better indicator than age is whether the child is asking for more soccer and outgrowing the challenge level of rec play.'),
('Soccer', 'en', 'What is MLS NEXT, and how does a player get into it?', 'MLS NEXT is the top elite youth development platform in the U.S. and Canada (ages U13-U19), run in partnership with MLS academies and top independent clubs; it replaced the U.S. Soccer Development Academy in 2020. Entry is club-based, not individual: a player has to be identified and rostered by a club that holds an MLS NEXT license, typically through club tryouts, guest-playing, or being scouted from another team.'),
('Soccer', 'en', 'What does the path to professional soccer (MLS, USL, overseas) look like?', 'The most direct route is being identified inside an MLS academy and offered an MLS NEXT Pro or first-team contract, sometimes as young as 16-17, which lets a player turn pro without attending college. The far more common route, even for very good players, is college soccer (often at a high-major D1 program) followed by the MLS SuperDraft or a trial with a USL/lower-division club, or moves to clubs overseas through open trials or agent networks. For most families, planning for college recruitment is the more realistic and lower-risk plan, with a direct pro pathway treated as a possible bonus outcome rather than the primary plan.'),
('Soccer', 'en', 'What do college soccer coaches actually look for, and when should recruiting start?', 'Coaches prioritize game speed, decision-making under pressure, and performance against strong opponents over highlight-reel skill moves; they primarily watch players live at showcases, ECNL/MLS NEXT events, and ID camps rather than relying on video alone. Serious recruiting conversations often begin during a player''s sophomore year of high school (in line with NCAA contact rules), so building a basic player profile, schedule of tournaments, and a shortlist of target schools by freshman/sophomore year is common practice for players aiming at D1 or D2 soccer.'),
('Soccer', 'en', 'What''s the difference between MLS NEXT, ECNL, and Girls Academy as development pathways?', 'ECNL (Elite Clubs National League) is a long-running elite league, especially dominant on the girls'' side, that many top college recruits come through and that runs its own national showcases with heavy college-coach attendance. Girls Academy (GA) is a newer competing top-tier girls'' platform formed by clubs that left ECNL, also drawing strong college interest. MLS NEXT is the boys''-dominant (though it has some girls'' programming) platform tied most directly to MLS club academies and the professional pathway. None of the three is strictly "better" for every player — regional strength, specific club quality, and which platform''s showcases a family''s target colleges actually attend matter more than the league name itself.'),
('Soccer', 'en', 'Should a promising young player consider trialing with or moving to a club academy overseas?', 'It''s realistic only for a very small number of exceptional players, usually identified by scouts or agents rather than families cold-applying, and it comes with real tradeoffs: leaving school, family, and a normal childhood environment as a young teenager, uncertain visa/work-permit rules depending on the country, and no guarantee of a contract even after a trial goes well. Most development experts recommend exhausting domestic elite pathways (MLS academy, ECNL/GA, national team ID camps) first and treating an overseas move as something that should be initiated by credible outside interest, not sought out unprompted.'),
('Soccer', 'en', 'What position-specific skills matter most at different ages?', 'Through roughly U10-U12, coaches want broad ball mastery and comfort playing multiple positions rather than early specialization into a single role. From U13 onward, as tactical understanding grows, position-specific demands sharpen — for example, first-touch under pressure and scanning for center-mids, 1v1 defending and recovery speed for outside backs, and finishing efficiency and movement off the ball for forwards — but even at the elite youth level, versatility (being able to competently play more than one position) is still valued by college and academy coaches because it makes a player more useful to a roster.'),
('Soccer', 'en', 'What role does futsal play in a young player''s development?', 'Futsal (indoor small-sided soccer with a low-bounce ball) is widely credited by developers, including in Brazil''s and Spain''s youth systems, with accelerating close control, quick decision-making, and comfort under pressure because of the smaller space and higher touch count per player. Many U.S. clubs now use futsal as a winter or supplemental activity for players as young as 6-7, and it''s a reasonable lower-cost, lower-time-commitment alternative or complement to full 11v11 travel soccer at younger ages.'),
('Soccer', 'en', 'How does the U.S. youth national team pathway work, and how does a player get noticed for it?', 'U.S. Soccer identifies players for youth national teams (U14 through U20 boys'' and girls'' pools) primarily through scouts attending MLS NEXT, ECNL, and Girls Academy showcases, ID camps run by U.S. Soccer or regional development programs, and club/academy coach recommendations, rather than an open tryout system most families can simply enter. Making a youth national team pool is a strong signal for college and even direct pro/academy interest, but it represents a very small number of players nationally and is not required to have a successful college or academy career.'),
('Soccer', 'en', 'What showcases or events matter most for exposure?', 'For boys, MLS NEXT Cup/Fest events and top regional MLS academy showcases draw the heaviest college and pro scouting; for girls, ECNL Playoffs/Finals and Girls Academy national events play the same role. Generation adidas Cup and various regional Development Academy-successor showcases are also well attended by college staffs. Families should confirm, ideally directly with target college programs, which specific events those coaches actually plan to attend that season, since attendance can shift year to year.'),
('Soccer', 'en', 'What''s the difference between MLS NEXT (youth) and MLS NEXT Pro?', 'MLS NEXT is the youth development league (U13-U19); MLS NEXT Pro is a separate professional third-division league (below MLS) launched in 2022 specifically to bridge academy players into professional soccer, along with reserve teams from MLS first-team rosters. A standout MLS NEXT youth player can be signed to an MLS NEXT Pro contract as young as 16-17, making it the most direct non-college route to professional soccer in the U.S. system.'),
('Soccer', 'en', 'How important is raw speed/athletic testing compared to technical skill for recruiting?', 'Technical and tactical ability (first touch, passing accuracy, game reading) remains the primary evaluation criterion at every level through college soccer, unlike sports such as football or track where testing numbers are central. That said, measurable athleticism — sprint speed, repeated-sprint ability, and physical duels — increasingly separates similarly skilled players as they move toward higher levels, and college coaches do informally clock speed and note physical duels during live evaluation even without formal combine-style testing.'),
('Basketball', 'en', 'When should a child start playing AAU or travel basketball, and does the specific team matter?', 'Most kids can start AAU/travel ball around ages 9-11 once they have basic ballhandling and understand team concepts; before that, house leagues, camps, and skill training generally serve development better than travel schedules. At the youngest ages, the specific team matters far less than getting quality coaching and enough playing time to develop; by high school age, team quality and exposure start to matter much more for anyone hoping to be recruited.'),
('Basketball', 'en', 'What are the "shoe circuits" (Nike EYBL, Adidas 3SSB, Under Armour Association, Puma Pro16, New Balance) and how does a player get on one?', 'These are invitation-only elite travel circuits sponsored by shoe brands, widely regarded as the premier college and NBA scouting platforms in the country; Nike''s EYBL and its Peach Jam finale are generally considered the top event. Players do not apply or try out in an open sense — teams'' coaching staffs recruit players directly, usually based on standout performance at the high school level or on a strong independent/regional circuit team. These circuits are aimed at the top fraction of a percent of players nationally already drawing high-major D1 interest.'),
('Basketball', 'en', 'How does college basketball recruiting typically unfold?', 'Coaches build shortlists from live evaluation at AAU events, high school games, and team camps, cross-referenced with call-in/text contact once NCAA rules allow it (generally beginning June 15 after sophomore year). Unofficial visits (family-funded) can happen earlier; official visits (school-funded, capped at five total in most sports) typically happen junior/senior year. Verbal commitments increasingly happen earlier than they used to, but nothing is binding until a National Letter of Intent or financial aid agreement is signed.'),
('Basketball', 'en', 'What is the path to professional basketball (NBA, G League, overseas)?', 'The traditional route is one or more years of college basketball followed by entering the NBA Draft (players must be 19 and one year removed from high school graduation to be draft-eligible). Alternative routes that have grown in recent years include G League development contracts, playing professionally overseas directly out of high school, and using NIL deals to earn income while still in college. For the vast majority of serious high school players, college basketball, at whatever division fits their level, remains the more realistic and financially sound next step.'),
('Basketball', 'en', 'Does AAU matter more than high school ball for college exposure, or vice versa?', 'AAU/travel ball is generally where the heaviest college recruiting exposure happens, because it concentrates many strong teams and evaluators into a small number of weekends, but high school performance and team success still matter, particularly for programs recruiting more regionally or for players outside the very top national tier. The strongest recruiting profiles typically combine both: standout high school production plus performance against elite competition on the AAU circuit.'),
('Basketball', 'en', 'How do international pathways (NBL Next Stars, overseas pro leagues) compare to college basketball for elite U.S. prospects?', 'Programs like Australia''s NBL Next Stars or direct professional contracts overseas let a tiny number of elite, NBA-Draft-ready 18-year-olds get paid and train professionally for a year instead of playing college ball, and several have gone on to be lottery picks. For the large majority of even very good high school players, this route isn''t realistically available or advisable — it''s aimed specifically at consensus one-and-done-level NBA prospects, and college basketball (now paired with NIL income) offers most players better development, exposure, and financial terms.'),
('Basketball', 'en', 'How much do recruiting-ranking services (247Sports, Rivals, ESPN) actually matter?', 'These services aggregate scout evaluations into star ratings and national rankings that many coaches, media, and families reference as shorthand, and a high ranking can genuinely increase recruiting attention and offers. However, rankings are compiled by a relatively small number of evaluators and are known to have real subjectivity and regional bias, so families should treat a ranking as one data point (useful mainly for visibility) rather than a precise measure of a player''s actual college readiness or ceiling.'),
('Basketball', 'en', 'How does the college transfer portal affect roster spots and opportunities for incoming high school recruits?', 'Because portal players are experienced and immediately eligible, many programs now fill a meaningful share of their roster needs through the transfer portal rather than the incoming freshman class, which can mean fewer available freshman scholarship spots at some schools in a given cycle than in years past. It''s a reasonable, direct question to ask a recruiting coach: how many freshman spots do they expect to have open that year versus fill through the portal.'),
('Basketball', 'en', 'How does the pathway differ for girls'' basketball, including the route to the WNBA?', 'The overall structure (AAU/travel circuits, high school, college recruiting) is similar for boys and girls, though the girls'' shoe-circuit ecosystem is somewhat less developed and Nike, Adidas, and other AAU circuits for girls carry slightly different relative weight than the boys'' side. The WNBA draft pool is much smaller than the NBA''s (fewer teams, fewer roster spots), so the realistic post-college outcome for most serious college players, even very good ones, is often playing professionally overseas rather than in the WNBA, at least for the first several years of a pro career.'),
('Basketball', 'en', 'What are the key national camps/events for basketball exposure beyond the shoe circuits?', 'Nike Basketball Skills Academies (position-specific, invitation-based), the adidas Gauntlet, and various college team camps (where prospects attend a specific school''s camp to be evaluated directly by that staff) are widely attended by college and, in some cases, NBA personnel. High school all-star events (McDonald''s All American Game and similar) sit at the very top for a tiny number of elite senior prospects.'),
('Basketball', 'en', 'What physical testing do college programs actually look at?', 'Unlike football, basketball recruiting doesn''t rely on formal combine numbers for most recruits, but coaches do informally track vertical leap, lateral quickness, wingspan, and sprint speed during live viewings, and top prospects are sometimes measured more formally at combine-style events (like those run alongside major camps). These numbers matter more as a tiebreaker between similarly skilled players, or for identifying an athletic "upside" project, than as a primary evaluation tool.'),
('Basketball', 'en', 'How young is too young for a verbal commitment, and does it mean anything binding?', 'Verbal commitments have crept younger over the past decade, including some in 8th or 9th grade for elite prospects, though this remains controversial among coaches and recruiting analysts as premature given how much a player''s level and a program''s needs can change over 3-4 years. A verbal commitment is not binding on either side — it''s a public statement of mutual intent — and the only binding step is signing a National Letter of Intent or financial aid agreement, which typically happens senior year.'),
('Tennis', 'en', 'How do USTA rankings and UTR (Universal Tennis Rating) differ, and which one matters more?', 'USTA rankings are age-group specific and based on results within USTA-sanctioned tournaments in a junior''s own section and age division; they determine tournament entry and seeding. UTR is an age-independent rating (roughly 1-16.5) based on actual match results against any opponent, and it has become the primary tool college coaches use to compare players objectively across regions and age groups. Both matter, but UTR has become the more universal recruiting "currency," while USTA ranking still governs which tournaments a junior can enter.'),
('Tennis', 'en', 'What UTR level is realistic for different college divisions?', 'As rough benchmarks (these shift year to year and by gender), Division III and lower Division II programs often recruit in the UTR 6-9 range; mid-to-upper Division II typically looks for roughly UTR 8-11 (men) and 6-9 (women); competitive Division I programs generally want UTR 10+ (women) to 12+ (men), with power-conference programs recruiting well above that and often looking at national Top 100-150 junior rankings or ITF junior points as well. UTR only improves by playing and winning matches, so tournament match volume matters more than practice volume alone for this specific number.'),
('Tennis', 'en', 'When should a young player start entering tournaments, and when does recruiting start?', 'Local and low-level USTA tournaments are common starting around age 8-10 for kids already taking lessons seriously; sectional and national-level events typically follow in the young teens as a player''s level rises. Because Division I coaches can''t initiate contact until June 15 after sophomore year, families aiming at college tennis typically start building a UTR/results history, a highlight video, and a target-school list by freshman or sophomore year of high school.'),
('Tennis', 'en', 'What is the path to going professional in tennis?', 'The typical elite pathway runs through ITF Junior tournaments (juniors world rankings), then a mix of ITF World Tennis Tour and Challenger events to earn ATP/WTA ranking points, with a small number of exceptionally high-ranked juniors turning pro directly out of juniors rather than playing college tennis. Many strong players who are not quite at that top-20-in-the-world junior level use college tennis as a development and exposure step before deciding whether to try the professional tour, since only a small fraction of touring pros turn a profit in their early years.'),
('Tennis', 'en', 'Is it worth sending a child to a full-time tennis academy (like IMG) versus training locally with a coach?', 'Full-time boarding academies offer daily high-level training, sparring partners of similar caliber, and academic schedules built around tennis, which can accelerate development for a player already performing at a high national or international junior level. It''s a major financial and lifestyle commitment (tuition often comparable to or exceeding private college costs, plus being away from home as a young teenager), and it isn''t necessary for most strong juniors — many successful D1 and even touring players trained locally with a good coach and simply traveled for tournaments.'),
('Tennis', 'en', 'How does the ITF Junior circuit and the junior Grand Slams fit into a player''s development?', 'The ITF Junior circuit is the main international pathway below the pro tours, culminating in the four junior Grand Slam events (Australian Open, French Open, Wimbledon, US Open juniors); a strong ITF junior ranking (roughly top 50-100 in the world) is a meaningful signal to both college coaches and professional-tour talent scouts. It''s optional for players aiming primarily at college tennis, but essentially required for anyone seriously considering turning pro directly out of juniors.'),
('Tennis', 'en', 'What does the recruiting visit process look like specifically for tennis?', 'Similar to other sports, tennis recruiting includes unofficial visits (family-funded, can happen anytime) and a limited number of official visits (school-funded, generally capped and usable once contact rules allow it, junior/senior year); because roster sizes are small (often 8-10 players per team), coaches often make firm decisions on a handful of targets earlier and more decisively than in larger-roster sports, so responsiveness and an accurate UTR/ranking history matter a lot in a short recruiting window.'),
('Tennis', 'en', 'How does the college tennis format (team dual matches) differ from individual junior tournament tennis?', 'College tennis is scored as team dual matches (typically 3 doubles matches worth 1 combined point, then 6 singles matches each worth 1 point, first to 4 wins the dual), which rewards being a solid, consistent contributor across a lineup rather than only being an individual-tournament standout. Coaches specifically evaluate how a recruit''s game translates to both a doubles role and a fixed singles lineup position, which is a different lens than pure individual junior ranking.'),
('Tennis', 'en', 'What''s the financial reality of tennis scholarships — are they usually full rides?', 'Men''s and women''s college tennis are "equivalency" sports at the NCAA D1 level, meaning a set number of scholarships is split across a roster rather than every player getting a full ride, so most tennis scholarships are partial, not full. Combined with academic merit aid, a partial athletic scholarship can still meaningfully reduce total cost, but families should not assume recruitment automatically means a full scholarship, even at a competitive D1 program.'),
('Tennis', 'en', 'How important is doubles ability for recruiting, compared to singles ranking?', 'Very important and often underweighted by junior players themselves: college dual-match scoring gives doubles real weight (1 full point out of 7), so coaches specifically look for recruits who are competent doubles players, not just strong singles competitors, and a player with a so-so singles ranking but excellent doubles instincts can still be a valuable recruit.'),
('Tennis', 'en', 'What is a wild card, and how does it work for a young player trying to break into pro tournaments?', 'A wild card is a tournament entry granted outside the normal ranking-based qualification process, often given to promising young or local players to gain match experience against higher-ranked professionals; national federations (like the USTA) and tournaments themselves control wild card allocation, and it''s typically earned through strong results in lower-level pro or top junior events rather than requested directly by a family.'),
('Tennis', 'en', 'How should a family balance tournament travel costs against actual development value?', 'Because UTR and ranking only move from match play, there''s real pressure to enter many tournaments, but travel, entry fees, and coaching-travel costs add up quickly, often exceeding other sports'' costs per year at the competitive level. A generally sound approach is prioritizing quality of competition (playing up or entering slightly higher-level draws when ready) over sheer tournament quantity, since a smaller number of well-chosen, competitive matches usually improves UTR and readiness more than a high volume of lopsided matches.'),
('Hockey', 'en', 'What is USA Hockey''s American Development Model (ADM), and what do the age stages mean?', 'The ADM is USA Hockey''s long-term athlete development framework, introduced in 2009, that structures training and competition by developmental stage rather than pushing early travel-team intensity. Age groups are organized as 6U, 8U, 10U, 12U, 14U, 16U (and 18U/Juniors beyond that), with cross-ice or half-ice play, station-based practices, and multi-sport emphasis at the youngest levels, shifting to full-ice, more position-specific and tactical work as players move through 12U-16U.'),
('Hockey', 'en', 'When should a player move from house league to travel or AAA hockey?', 'Most associations offer travel options starting around 8U-10U; AAA (the highest tier) typically becomes relevant from 12U onward, once puberty-related size and skill differences start to separate players more clearly. As with other sports, the ADM philosophy discourages rushing a young child into elite-tier travel purely for the label, favoring broad skill-building and, ideally, multi-sport participation through the 10U-12U range.'),
('Hockey', 'en', 'How does the path through juniors work, and how does it interact with NCAA eligibility?', 'After high school-age travel/AAA hockey, the main junior pathways are USHL, NAHL, and USPHL in the U.S. (NCAA-eligible "amateur" leagues) or the Canadian major-junior leagues under the CHL umbrella (WHL, OHL, QMJHL). A major 2025 NCAA rule change now allows CHL players to keep Division I eligibility starting in the 2025-26 season, as long as they aren''t paid beyond actual and necessary expenses — this reverses decades of CHL players being treated as ineligible for NCAA hockey, though the change does not apply to Division III, where CHL players remain ineligible. Most players headed for college hockey still play junior hockey at ages 18-20 even after high school graduation, since NCAA hockey rosters skew older than most other college sports.'),
('Hockey', 'en', 'What is the path to professional hockey (NHL, AHL, ECHL)?', 'The primary route is being drafted into the NHL (draft-eligible at 18) out of major junior or U.S. college hockey, followed by additional development in the AHL before an NHL roster spot. A player can also sign an NHL entry-level contract undrafted, and many careers include several years bouncing between AHL/ECHL and brief NHL call-ups. The number of youth travel/AAA players who ultimately play a single NHL game is a very small fraction of a percent, making college hockey and the enjoyment of the sport itself the realistic primary goals for the large majority of families.'),
('Hockey', 'en', 'What is prep school hockey, and what is the USA Hockey National Team Development Program (NTDP)?', 'Northeast prep schools (many in New England) have long been a strong development and college-exposure route, combining a rigorous academic environment with high-level hockey and heavy college-coach attendance at games. The NTDP, based in Plymouth, Michigan, is USA Hockey''s centralized program for the country''s top 16-17-year-old prospects, who play a demanding U17/U18 schedule against elite competition (including NCAA and international teams) and is a primary pipeline into the NHL Draft''s first round and top NCAA programs; entry is by invitation following an extensive national scouting and camp process, not open application.'),
('Hockey', 'en', 'How does the pathway differ for girls'' hockey, including the route to professional play?', 'The youth-through-college structure is broadly similar, with travel/AAA teams, prep schools, and NCAA women''s hockey (a fully scholarship-funded D1 sport) as the main development ladder, plus a girls''-specific USA Hockey national team development pathway. Professional opportunities have expanded significantly with the Professional Women''s Hockey League (PWHL), formed in 2023-24, which now offers a viable — though still roster-limited — pro pathway after college, alongside longstanding Olympic and IIHF World Championship national-team opportunities.'),
('Hockey', 'en', 'What is a "tender" in junior hockey, and how does it work?', 'A tender is a binding commitment a junior team (commonly in the USHL or NAHL) offers a player, guaranteeing a roster spot and effectively taking the player out of that year''s league draft in exchange for the player agreeing to report to that team. It''s a significant step for a family to evaluate carefully, since it can lock in a specific team/location for junior hockey, and families should understand exactly what it commits them to before accepting one.'),
('Hockey', 'en', 'What showcases matter most for hockey exposure?', 'Events like the USA Hockey All-American Prospects Game, World Selects Invite, and various NAHL/USHL League and Combine showcases draw heavy scouting from junior teams, NCAA programs, and NHL Central Scouting. Regional and state Tier 1 tournaments also matter, but the multi-team showcase format (many teams playing in one location over a few days) is generally what draws the widest, most efficient evaluator attendance.'),
('Hockey', 'en', 'What does the NHL Draft process and combine actually involve?', 'Players become NHL Draft-eligible the year they turn 18, and NHL Central Scouting tracks and ranks prospects (North American skaters, goalies, and European players separately) throughout their draft-eligible season based on extensive in-person scouting of junior, NCAA, and international games. Top prospects are invited to the NHL Scouting Combine, which includes fitness testing (VO2 max, strength, agility) and formal team interviews, though on-ice performance and hockey sense evaluated over a full season still weigh more heavily than combine testing numbers in most draft decisions.'),
('Hockey', 'en', 'How much does size/physicality matter compared to skill in hockey evaluation?', 'Pure skill, skating ability, and hockey sense (reading the play, anticipation) are still the top evaluation criteria at every serious level, but hockey remains a physical, contact sport, so size and the ability to compete physically do matter more here than in a sport like soccer or tennis, particularly for defensemen and depth forward roles. A smaller player with elite skill and skating is still highly recruitable, but is often specifically evaluated on whether that skill translates against bigger, more physical competition as the level rises.'),
('Hockey', 'en', 'What is the realistic cost of pursuing elite youth hockey, and are there ways to manage it?', 'Hockey is among the more expensive youth sports once equipment, ice time, travel team fees, and tournament travel are included, commonly reaching several thousand to over ten thousand dollars per year at AAA or prep-school levels. Families can manage cost by prioritizing ice time and skill development (private skills sessions, skating-specific coaching) over sheer number of tournaments, using association-run or non-shoe-affiliated travel programs rather than the most expensive AAA options when a child is younger, and researching need-based financial aid at prep schools and college programs, which is often more available than families assume.'),
('Baseball', 'en', 'When should a player move into travel ball, and how important are showcases?', 'Travel ball typically starts around 8U-10U, though many development experts caution against year-round single-sport travel baseball before puberty due to overuse arm injuries. Showcases (Perfect Game, Prep Baseball Report, and similar) become central for exposure starting around age 14-15, since velocity, exit velocity, and 60-yard times are directly measurable and heavily used by both college recruiters and pro scouts.'),
('Baseball', 'en', 'How does the MLB Draft interact with college recruiting?', 'Uniquely among major U.S. sports, baseball allows a player to be drafted by MLB straight out of high school, then choose between signing professionally or attending college; NCAA players become draft-eligible again after their junior year or once they turn 21. This creates a real decision point around "signability" that doesn''t exist in most other sports, and drafted high schoolers often negotiate bonus terms with advisors before deciding whether to sign or enroll in college.'),
('Baseball', 'en', 'What arm-care and pitch-count guidelines should parents know about?', 'Youth pitching injuries (especially UCL/Tommy John-related) have risen sharply with year-round travel ball, and USA Baseball/MLB pitch-smart guidelines recommend specific daily and weekly pitch-count limits by age along with mandatory rest days between outings; following these guidelines, and avoiding pitching on multiple teams in overlapping seasons, is one of the most evidence-backed injury-prevention steps a family can take.'),
('American Football', 'en', 'How does football recruiting differ from sports with youth travel circuits?', 'Recruiting is dominated by high school performance, camps/combines (including "one-day" college camps and events like The Opening), and recruiting-service star ratings, more than club/travel circuits, since there is no true travel-ball equivalent before high school. Because football rosters are large and needs are highly position-specific, exposure at position-specific camps and 7-on-7 events matters more than in most other sports covered here.'),
('American Football', 'en', 'What role do redshirting and the transfer portal play in football specifically?', 'Football uses redshirting more than any other sport, given large roster sizes and the physical demands of the position; a true freshman redshirting to add strength and learn the system is common and not a setback. The transfer portal has also reshaped football roster-building significantly (with a set January 2-16 signing window as of 2026), meaning some programs fill more roster needs through experienced transfers than in past recruiting cycles, which is worth asking about directly.'),
('American Football', 'en', 'At what age should tackle football start, and are there safer alternatives early on?', 'Given concussion and repetitive-hit research, many pediatric and youth-sport organizations now recommend delaying tackle football until age 12-14 and using flag football for skill development before that; flag football also builds the same route-running, ball-handling, and field-awareness skills without the repetitive-impact exposure of youth tackle leagues.'),
('Swimming', 'en', 'How does recruiting work in a sport with no subjective judging?', 'Swimming is one of the most objectively measurable recruiting sports: college coaches recruit almost entirely off USA Swimming times relative to published team/conference standards, not off subjective evaluation, which makes a swimmer''s own time-standard sheet the single most important recruiting document.'),
('Swimming', 'en', 'Where does competitive swimming development actually happen — club or high school?', 'Age-group swimming (via USA Swimming clubs, not primarily high school teams) is where competitive times are built and where year-round training volume and technique work occur; high school swimming is often a lower-volume, team-spirit-focused season layered on top of club training rather than the primary development pathway.'),
('Swimming', 'en', 'When does recruiting typically happen, given swimmers often peak later?', 'Because swimming performance often peaks later than in other sports, recruiting conversations frequently continue meaningfully into junior and senior year based on a swimmer''s improvement trajectory, not just current times, so a swimmer who isn''t yet hitting target times as a sophomore is not necessarily out of contention for strong programs.'),
('Gymnastics', 'en', 'Is early specialization actually necessary in gymnastics, unlike most other sports?', 'Yes — gymnastics is a genuine exception to the "don''t specialize early" guidance: elite-level artistic gymnastics generally requires specialization well before puberty because peak competitive readiness (particularly for women''s elite/international level) often occurs in the mid-to-late teens, before significant growth-related changes in body mechanics.'),
('Gymnastics', 'en', 'How does the level system (Levels 1-10, then Elite) work?', 'USA Gymnastics'' compulsory/optional level system (Levels 1-10) structures progression with standardized skill requirements at each level before advancing, followed by the Elite level for those pursuing national team and international competition; most gymnasts, including many who go on to compete in college, progress through Levels 8-10 rather than reaching Elite.'),
('Gymnastics', 'en', 'Does gymnastics offer a genuine college scholarship pathway even without reaching Elite?', 'Yes — women''s college gymnastics is a fully scholarship-funded NCAA sport (a headcount-equivalency mix depending on division), and the large majority of college gymnasts came through the Level 10 club pathway rather than the national Elite team, making college gymnastics a realistic and common goal for talented non-Elite club gymnasts.'),
('Volleyball', 'en', 'Does club or high school volleyball matter more for recruiting?', 'Club volleyball (not high school volleyball) is the primary vehicle for college recruiting exposure in most regions, especially via events sanctioned by JVA and AAU; recruiting exposure camps and "club nationals" tournaments draw the heaviest college-coach attendance.'),
('Volleyball', 'en', 'Are late bloomers common in volleyball recruiting?', 'Yes — given growth-related advantages in height and reach, late bloomers are common and often still competitive for college recruiting since jump height and vertical explosiveness can develop significantly through the mid-to-late teens, so a shorter or less explosive 14-year-old is not necessarily out of contention by 17-18.'),
('Volleyball', 'en', 'What positions get recruited differently, and does specialization matter?', 'Colleges recruit by position (outside hitter, middle blocker, setter, libero, etc.), and by the mid-teens most serious club players do specialize by position, since the skill sets diverge significantly (a setter''s ball-handling versus a middle''s blocking timing, for example); this is one of the more position-specialized recruiting processes among the sports covered here.'),
('Track', 'en', 'How does recruiting work, and does the athlete''s team or region matter?', 'Like swimming, recruiting is largely times-and-marks based and comparable across programs regardless of which team or region an athlete competes for, which makes independent training and smaller programs less of a disadvantage than in sports reliant on team exposure circuits.'),
('Track', 'en', 'When do distance runners typically peak, and how should that affect expectations?', 'Distance runners in particular tend to reach competitive peaks later (late teens to early-to-mid 20s) than athletes in most other sports covered here, so patience with a longer development timeline, and avoiding excessive early-teen training volume, is more the norm than in sports like gymnastics or figure skating.'),
('Golf', 'en', 'When should a young golfer start competing, and what''s the path to college recruiting?', 'Junior tournament golf through organizations like the AJGA (American Junior Golf Association), along with state and regional junior tours, is the primary path to exposure, with serious competitive play typically starting in the early-to-mid teens as a player''s scoring average becomes reliable; college coaches recruit heavily off a player''s tournament scoring history and AJGA/national junior ranking rather than a single event.'),
('Golf', 'en', 'What''s the path to professional golf?', 'The dominant pathway is college golf (a strong men''s or women''s D1 program), followed by mini-tours and Q-School/PGA Tour University or LPGA''s equivalent qualifying pathways; very few golfers turn professional directly out of junior golf, and college golf is generally seen as essential development time even for players who eventually turn pro.'),
('Lacrosse', 'en', 'How does club/travel lacrosse recruiting compare to other sports?', 'Club lacrosse recruiting follows a similar pattern to soccer and volleyball: regional and national tournaments and showcases (run by circuits like the recruiting-focused club and high school all-star events) are where most serious college exposure happens, with recruiting conversations often starting notably early relative to other sports — verbal commitments in 9th-10th grade have historically been common, though NCAA rules and recent scrutiny have pushed some of that timeline later.'),
('Lacrosse', 'en', 'Is lacrosse a good option for late-starting athletes?', 'Yes, more than many sports — because organized youth lacrosse is regionally concentrated (strongest in the Northeast, Mid-Atlantic, and pockets elsewhere) rather than universal, athletically gifted players who start in middle school or even early high school, often transferring skills from other sports, are more common in lacrosse recruiting than in sports like gymnastics or tennis where a late start is a bigger handicap.'),
('Wrestling', 'en', 'How does individual performance translate into college recruiting for wrestling?', 'Wrestling recruiting is driven heavily by objective results — state placement, national high school rankings, and results at major youth/folkstyle, freestyle, and Greco-Roman tournaments (like Fargo/USA Wrestling nationals) — making it one of the more purely merit-based, results-transparent recruiting processes among the sports covered here.'),
('Wrestling', 'en', 'What''s the path to elite/Olympic wrestling versus college wrestling?', 'College wrestling (NCAA D1/D2/D3 and NAIA) is the main development step for nearly all serious American wrestlers, including future Olympians, since USA Wrestling''s senior national team and Olympic pathway is drawn overwhelmingly from current and former college wrestlers rather than a separate youth-to-pro track; there is no direct "turn pro out of high school" route comparable to baseball or basketball.'),
(null, 'en', 'My child needs to specialize by age 10 to have a shot at college or pro sports, right?', 'False for the vast majority of sports. Multi-sport participation through early adolescence is linked to better outcomes and lower injury risk in soccer, basketball, tennis, hockey, baseball, football, and volleyball. Early specialization is a genuine requirement mainly in gymnastics and figure skating.'),
(null, 'en', 'If my child isn''t on the top travel team by age 10, have they missed their window?', 'False. Growth timing (see peak height velocity) varies by years between kids of the same birth year, and "late bloomers" regularly overtake early-maturing peers by mid-to-late high school. Team placement at 9-10 years old has very little predictive value for ability at 16-18.'),
(null, 'en', 'Can a recruiting service or private showcase get my child a scholarship?', 'No third party can guarantee a roster spot or scholarship; only a college coaching staff makes that decision, based on need and fit. Paid services can provide useful organization and video editing, but families should verify independently which college coaches actually attend any camp before paying for access.'),
(null, 'en', 'Is more practice always better for a young athlete?', 'Not past a point. Overuse injury and burnout risk rise sharply with high year-round single-sport volume, and pediatric guidance recommends built-in weekly rest days and multi-month seasonal breaks even for committed young athletes.'),
(null, 'en', 'Does a verbal commitment mean my child is locked into that school?', 'False — a verbal commitment is a mutual, non-binding statement of intent, not a contract. Either side can back out before signing a National Letter of Intent or financial aid agreement, and it isn''t uncommon for coaching changes, roster needs, or a player''s own development to change plans well after a verbal commitment.'),
(null, 'en', 'Does the transfer portal mean my child can just leave a college program if they''re unhappy, with no real cost?', 'Overly simple. While transfer rules have loosened considerably, transferring still typically means adjusting to a new team, system, and academic environment, may affect eligibility timing depending on the sport and situation, and isn''t guaranteed to result in a better scholarship, role, or fit than the original program.'),
(null, 'en', 'Does getting recruited automatically mean a full-ride scholarship?', 'False in most sports. Only football and basketball at the D1 level are "headcount" sports with full scholarships for every roster spot; nearly every other sport (soccer, tennis, hockey, baseball, volleyball, etc.) is an "equivalency" sport where a limited scholarship pool is split, often partially, across the roster.'),
(null, 'en', 'Does my child need to play up an age group to be properly challenged?', 'Sometimes true, often overdone. Playing up occasionally against strong competition can help a clearly advanced player, but doing it constantly, especially before puberty, raises injury and burnout risk from facing bigger, stronger, more physically mature opponents, and can undermine confidence without a clear developmental payoff.'),
(null, 'en', 'Does bigger and faster always beat more skilled, at least until college?', 'Not reliably true, and it flips at higher levels. Physical maturity gives a real edge at younger ages, which is why early-maturing kids often dominate youth sports, but as competition levels rise (especially at the college and professional level) technical skill, decision-making, and game-sense-type qualities become the deciding factors, which is part of why many "late bloomer" technically skilled players overtake early-maturing peers in the teens.'),
(null, 'en', 'Once a young athlete is injured, does any college interest disappear?', 'Usually false for a single, well-managed injury with full medical clearance. Coaches evaluate documented recovery and objective return-to-play testing, not just injury history in isolation; many current college athletes, including scholarship players, have a significant injury somewhere in their background.');
